v3.7.4
a registered image is pinned only when it is an enclave image the Ethernity
  CLOUD SDK builds (scone_image.py): its lib/libbinary-fs.so, the SCONE
  binary-fs, holds the SCONE runtime's variables and the enclave's role
  (securelock or trustedzone), found newest layer first within
  REGISTERED_IMAGE_SCAN_MAX_BYTES (1 GiB) of layer data, decompressing at
  most REGISTERED_IMAGE_INFLATE_MAX_BYTES (2 GiB) and 100,000 archive
  entries; its tree declares at most REGISTERED_IMAGE_MAX_BYTES (3 GiB) and
  its compose REGISTERED_COMPOSE_MAX_BYTES (1 MiB); and the disk keeps
  ESR_MIN_FREE_STORAGE_GB free before the pin, once the declared size is in,
  and while the pin runs; a pin that grows the repository past twice the
  declared size plus 2 GiB is ended and tried again later. Archives are walked
  by the agent itself, header by header, with extended headers over 1 MiB
  refused unread, so no layer makes it hold more than a few MiB
a verdict (verified, refused, unreadable by this agent) stands until those
  bounds or the verifier's version change; an image pinned without a verdict
  is judged in place; a verified image whose pin Kubo no longer holds is
  pinned again, and the retention sweep keeps verified images; a refusal
  unpins only what this replication pinned (pins made by 3.7.2 and 3.7.3 stay),
  sparing a CID another verified image of the network uses
an attempt that fails is tried again after a delay doubling from 10 minutes
  to 6 hours, the entry with the fewest attempts first; one registered-image
  attempt runs at a time in the process
a registered image's publisher is taken only with a canonical base58 peer id,
  added to the peering list only when it is not in it already, and only a
  peering the attempt added is removed after
a registered image and its compose are taken only as the CID text Kubo prints
  for them (varints as go-cid reads them, CIDv1 padding bits zero); an entry
  3.7.2 or 3.7.3 recorded under another spelling is refused, under whatever
  bounds are current
a registration whose strings do not decode is skipped alone
a replication-only agent collects the garbage of refused, unreadable and
  failed attempts at most hourly, while no registered-image pin is in flight
the handles of a network share one esr_progress, written as one snapshot that
  replaces the file
the replication loop runs for the life of the process: the daily restart of
  the order-processing threads stopped it for good

v3.7.0
the -unsafe trustedzones are networks of their own, bloxberg_testnet_unsafe
  and litvm_liteforge_unsafe: the chain and contracts of the network each is
  named after, and only that network's -unsafe trustedzones. A node runs one
  only when it is named: "all", "auto", the installer's AUTO and the legacy
  names select none. An -unsafe network lists only trustedzones ending in
  -unsafe and no other network lists one, or the agent does not start
a node that names a network and its -unsafe twin runs both as one handle on
  their shared protocol contract, with both networks' trustedzones and
  integration tests
litvm_liteforge's trustedzones are provisioned by LitVM's CAS set: the agent
  resolves the CAS from LitVM's validator registry, replicates its session
  registry and peers with its validators' IPFS nodes
the payload intake takes a network's requests under its -unsafe twin's name
  as well
the installer lists LitVM and the two -unsafe networks

v3.6.2
an order placement is simulated before it is sent, and a DO request the
  protocol contract refuses is skipped and cached instead of retried: on
  bloxberg a request whose metadata4 is the zero address cannot be placed
  by any node, and the node re-sent the reverting _placeOrder without end,
  paying gas each time and taking no other request
a placement that is mined but reverted is retried at most three times

v3.6.1
an RPC call on a keep-alive connection the RPC closed while idle is retried
  on a fresh one: web3's default retry missed requests' ConnectionError, so
  the first call after a quiet spell failed with "Remote end closed
  connection without response" (the integration tests' registry reads)

v3.6.0
a node runs the trustedzones whose platform requirement its integration
  tests show it meets: <NETWORK>_TRUSTEDZONE_IMAGES pairs each trustedzone
  with "dcap" (provisioned by a CAS) or "sgx" (the -unsafe testnet variants),
  and <NETWORK>_INTEGRATION_TEST_IMAGES (renamed from
  _INTEGRATION_TEST_IMAGE) lists the images tested
integration tests are tracked per image and a failed one is retried with a
  backoff from 10 minutes doubling to 6 hours, instead of only at the
  daily restart; a test whose image cannot be downloaded cleans up and
  counts as failed
a DO request is skipped, with the reason logged, when the trustedzone it
  names is not one the node runs or its image metadata is not v3;
  process_order refuses such an order after re-running the failed tests
the CAS is resolved only for a compose that names SCONE_CAS_ADDR
a DP request's metadata4 carries the trustedzones the node runs and the
  peer id of its IPFS node
pin retention skips a trustedzone the image registry does not hold
  instead of the whole cleanup round
IPFS_GATEWAY_URL is read from the key node/.env sets
removed the legacy /dev/isgx etny-pynithy composes from node/docker, with
  their Dockerfile, wrapper and result script; the agent used none of them

v3.5.0
fixed result pinning: retries never reconnected, so a stale connection
  flag failed every attempt against a healthy IPFS daemon
one failed pin/ls no longer disables pinning node-wide
serialised IPFS restarts across network threads (lock + 60s cooldown),
  which stops one thread's restart from breaking the others' calls
results that fail to pin are queued on disk and retried after an IPFS
  outage or a node restart, instead of being lost with the process
CAS endpoints are read from CasKeyStore rather than the registry, which
  no longer forwards them (requires a registry exposing keyStore())
3s RPC connect timeout, replacing 120s applied to both phases
HTTP sessions are closed when a network iteration ends
the shared task slot is acquired atomically, so two networks can no
  longer hold it at once or clear each other's ownership

